.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
/data/*
!/data/daily100/
# The contract-listing cache is committed on purpose. This container has been rolled back to an
# older filesystem snapshot four times, and each rollback discards whatever the caches had
# rebuilt -- so a cache that only lives on disk keeps paying its 104-minute re-listing cost.
# Git is the one store here that survives a rollback: `git reset --hard origin/<branch>` brings
# the listings back instantly. The bar cache stays ignored -- 37k files, far too many to commit.
!/data/cache/
/data/cache/*
!/data/cache/contracts/
# The bar cache is committed for the same reason, arrived at the hard way. Rollbacks #4 and #5
# both pinned it back to exactly 18294 contracts, so the run made zero net progress across six
# hours of uptime: everything fetched between a snapshot and the next rollback was discarded.
# These 18k+ files are the accumulated product of ~150 hours of 4-calls-per-minute fetching and
# cannot be re-earned quickly, which is worth more than the repo weight (101MB of JSON, and git
# packs it far smaller). Loose files rather than a tarball on purpose: git then stores each
# contract once and a later commit adds only the new ones, where a tarball would re-add the
# whole archive every time.
!/data/cache/bars/
.env
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.DS_Store
